s/4b7d6cca59677b4ec63fc9997d5d32c4d2e67f87d45b89540e42293f4ed1ba73.png) Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/146529d47426ad676072aeba20837a6da7ea5dc400a17f37dd3cb4d0a5c7f107.png)\nSo, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/f161fa211fd6dda217ad08855ed96703b8f620aeb1485d67562f3bf1666928d7.png) is a root of the equation. This means we can factor ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/210f39779d00613e2fcc094145f31e789ae93a0361618ae6b4623c107272799f.png) out of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/b8b832cc07ffad66665f2b961a9d78d97a0a89caac7ffc765e065a6d0946d0f6.png) using synthetic division:\n\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/124a84b4788cb014c01f416a1e0433eb7991fd3c4c792400892377f4f3407221.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/e12ac0c95825bef841bc95fa94b20bf787e03ec405c223a26fab4a73c61efa10.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/783e116511a2ce8a0ad54bedf9e79e7b8d380fc31dae50f43d1c4ce51a305354.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/cc8c6a49685acb757524d62a8cbb6805c5198485f888874bbbb66968aa9e6178.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/aeffa7b19c9d0bea2c544ec3cc1aa53ad8b44271b89f115e7a4db58ecfd33720.png) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/c84a10b5fd2ed823a1f173f8120dd2250031ad7e5f10424ef0d3cd91ec148bed.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/2f84eb7bb5c17949d98cacb1f217bbbd99ef39032a3a0a960412d0e94bcba8b6.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/df9b41873d2d60b97b90650e0713641a8647a38205427f8f8fe04cef9237646b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/ee595b41249fe71b2b1dbc2d31b9eb9069bd31e887f4bf62456ab05dd926318e.png) |\n|  | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/1baad26e6ade455fffeb4304606352ecfbf86e67e4cb47c35b736f59e44d009b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/af11b6c6dc78667e7d9c0d4acfeccea8ce8e6f83f49e3213083c8bff30c4fd14.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/30accb457d578da8be84827af0d559e9f57f0d18d08f8df490dc88879f8c90e7.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/278088cea7543d913bd6e9702b03c58ff40e05413a543120087373c426bad83e.png) |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/b7457336851f79803231c1d2d64a07591ff7bb5e1f3315dd8af98bfe2782bcb4.png)\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/6528f51e5d19fcda74b8ca490c4fd89b3e43269b57053da77b29284702c1f833.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/8c25f2182438cde814f01fd81ad6e94684ceed686db04002e7934b310e3261b1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/5e20e499636e1ce842b9d25a9934ce6956334c31f56d9cfd22f5230fbcbce983.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/example-5487.json" "file_sha256" "0b5e276d0b07bdf501dd975b6110809c2aadfe533197db39d9918ee6543712de"}] "usage" {"e-5486" [] "e-5487" [] "e-7047" [] "q-36845" [] "q-37355" []} "basis" 2359 "retractions" [[17592186124933 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186124978 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $λ =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n2. $λ =-10$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n3. The eigenvalue $λ =-10$ has algebraic multiplicity $3$"] [17592186124249 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124249 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 - λ & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - λ & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-9 - λ & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - λ & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-9 - λ)(9 - λ)(6 - λ) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 9)(λ - 9)(λ - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-9\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."] [17592186124298 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n2. $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n3. The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [17592186124298 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(P - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 - λ) \\cdot (4 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)((-1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 35) &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)(λ^{2} - 36) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)(λ - 4)(λ - 6)(λ + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3$, $\\lambda=4$, $\\lambda=6$, and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186124250 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124250 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}- (-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 - λ \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 17λ - 11) + (3 + λ) - 2(4 - 2λ) &= 0 \\\\\n- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 22λ - 16 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{3} + 5λ^{2} - 22λ + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16$:\n$\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16$\n\nLet's pick $x=1$. Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1)^{3} + 5(1)^{2} - 22(1) + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_2=2,\\,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_3=-8\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122621 17592186122622 17592186122623 17592186122624 17592186122625 17592186122891 17592186122892 17592186122893 17592186122894 17592186122895] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122620 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122620 :answer-field/id] [17592186122620 :answer-field/key] [17592186122620 :answer-field/type] [17592186122890 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122890 :answer-field/id] [17592186122890 :answer-field/key] [17592186122890 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
